[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Configuration Classes: Defining Beans and Dependencies in Spring Boot"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot leverages Java-based configuration to define beans and their dependencies. Configuration classes, annotated with `@Configuration`, are central to this approach. They serve as a source of bean definitions for the Spring application context. This tutorial will guide you through creating and using configuration classes to manage your application's components."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What are Configuration Classes?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A configuration class is a Java class annotated with `@Configuration`. Within this class, you define beans using the `@Bean` annotation. These methods act as bean factories, returning instances that Spring manages. Spring processes these classes during application startup and registers the returned objects as beans in the application context."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Configuration Class"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's create a simple configuration class that defines a `MessageService` bean."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public MessageService messageService() {
        return new MessageService("Hello, Spring!");
    }
}

class MessageService {
    private String message;

    public MessageService(String message) {
        this.message = message;
    }

    public String getMessage() {
        return message;
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `AppConfig` is annotated with `@Configuration`, indicating that it's a configuration class. The `messageService()` method is annotated with `@Bean`, which tells Spring to create and manage an instance of `MessageService` as a bean named "messageService"."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the Defined Bean"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can now inject the `MessageService` bean into other components using `@Autowired` or constructor injection."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MessagePrinter {

    private final MessageService messageService;

    @Autowired
    public MessagePrinter(MessageService messageService) {
        this.messageService = messageService;
    }

    public void printMessage() {
        System.out.println(messageService.getMessage());
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `MessagePrinter` depends on `MessageService`. Spring automatically injects the `MessageService` bean defined in `AppConfig` into the `MessagePrinter`'s constructor."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Bean Dependencies"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Configuration classes can also define dependencies between beans. This is achieved by calling other `@Bean` methods within the configuration class."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        return new DataSource("jdbc:mydb://localhost:3306/testdb");
    }

    @Bean
    public UserRepository userRepository(DataSource dataSource) {
        return new UserRepository(dataSource);
    }
}

class DataSource {
    private String url;

    public DataSource(String url) {
        this.url = url;
    }

    public String getUrl() {
        return url;
    }
}

class UserRepository {
    private DataSource dataSource;

    public UserRepository(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    public DataSource getDataSource() {
        return dataSource;
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `userRepository` depends on `dataSource`. The `userRepository` bean definition calls the `dataSource()` method to obtain an instance of `DataSource`, which is then passed as a constructor argument to `UserRepository`. Spring manages the creation and injection of the `DataSource` bean."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Using @Import"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@Import` annotation allows you to import configuration classes from other packages or modules. This helps in organizing your configuration into logical units."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Import;

@Configuration
@Import({DatabaseConfig.class, ServiceConfig.class})
public class AppConfig {

    // Other bean definitions

}

@Configuration
class DatabaseConfig {
    // Database related bean definitions
}

@Configuration
class ServiceConfig {
    // Service related bean definitions
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this case, `AppConfig` imports `DatabaseConfig` and `ServiceConfig`, effectively combining their bean definitions into a single application context."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Bean Creation with @Conditional"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes, you might want to create a bean only under certain conditions. The `@Conditional` annotation allows you to specify a condition that must be met for a bean to be created."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Conditional;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Condition;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.ConditionContext;
import org.springframework.core.type.AnnotatedTypeMetadata;

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    @Conditional(MyCondition.class)
    public MessageService messageService() {
        return new MessageService("Hello, Conditional Spring!");
    }
}

class MyCondition implements Condition {

    @Override
    public boolean matches(ConditionContext context, AnnotatedTypeMetadata metadata) {
        return context.getEnvironment().getProperty("my.property") != null;
    }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `MyCondition` implements the `Condition` interface and checks if the `my.property` is defined in the environment. The `messageService` bean will only be created if this condition is met."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Bean Scopes"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, Spring creates singleton beans. However, you can specify different scopes using the `@Scope` annotation. Common scopes include `prototype`, `singleton`, `request`, `session`, and `application`."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Scope;

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    @Scope("prototype")
    public MessageService messageService() {
        return new MessageService("Hello, Prototype!");
    }
}"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `messageService` is defined with the `prototype` scope.  This means that a new instance of `MessageService` will be created every time it's requested from the application context."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Configuration classes are a powerful mechanism for defining and managing beans in Spring Boot applications. They provide a clean and type-safe way to configure your application's components, define dependencies, and control bean creation based on various conditions. By leveraging `@Configuration`, `@Bean`, `@Import`, `@Conditional`, and `@Scope`, you can create flexible and maintainable Spring Boot applications."}]
